# Licence

IAC is licensed per artefact type, so that both specification implementers and studios
shipping commercial games can use it without legal friction.

| Path | Content | Licence | SPDX |
|---|---|---|---|
| `spec/`, `docs/`, `rfcs/`, `README*.md`, `GOVERNANCE.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `CHANGELOG.md` | Specification text and documentation | Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International | `CC-BY-4.0` |
| `tools/`, `.github/` | Reference implementation and CI configuration | Apache License 2.0 | `Apache-2.0` |
| `examples/`, `testdata/` | Example and conformance data files | CC0 1.0 Universal | `CC0-1.0` |

Copyright (c) 2026 MIAR STAFF and IAC contributors.

## Why this split

- **CC BY 4.0 on the specification** lets anyone quote, reprint, translate, and build on
  the normative text, including commercially, as long as attribution is preserved. It
  does not cover software, which is why the reference implementation is separate.
- **Apache-2.0 on the reference implementation** includes an explicit patent grant.
  Studios will not adopt a format whose reference parser has an unclear patent position.
- **CC0 on the data files** means a studio can copy an example cue sheet into a
  proprietary project with no attribution obligation and no licence audit.

## Attribution

When redistributing the specification or a derivative, credit:

> "IAC subtitle format specification, version 1.0, CC BY 4.0"

## Legal texts

The SPDX identifiers are authoritative. The full texts of CC BY 4.0, Apache-2.0, and
CC0-1.0 are not vendored into this repository; they are available from
[creativecommons.org](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/) and
[apache.org](https://www.apache.org/licenses/), and must be vendored before the
repository is published as an external release.

## Contributions

By submitting a pull request you agree to license your contribution under the licence of
the directory it lands in. See [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md).
